<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abel Tasman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1603–<a href="/years/1659">1659</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Abel Janszoon Tasman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abel Tasman was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> seafarer and explorer in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC), likely born in 1602 or 1603 in Lutjegast. On his voyage of 1642 he reached the west coast of Tasmania on 24 November, naming it Van Diemen's Land after his patron Anthony van Diemen, and then became the first European to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, which he named Staten Landt. Off what is now Golden Bay, Māori in a waka attacked a boat's crew and four sailors were killed, and Tasman named the place Murderers' Bay. A second voyage in 1644 mapped the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, but the company judged his explorations a disappointment. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in 1659.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abel-tasman.jpg" alt="Abel Tasman" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abel_Tasman_Navigateur_en_Australie_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tasman was uneducated but learned navigation and seamanship on the job after joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. He sailed from Texel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in 1633 by the southern Brouwer Route and was appointed skipper of the Mocha in 1634. He was second-in-command of a 1639 expedition under Matthijs Quast into the north Pacific, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-taiwan">Fort Zeelandia</a> on Formosa and Deshima off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. He married Jannetje Tjaers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1631, after an earlier marriage to Claesgie Heyndrix.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In August 1642 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> sent Tasman and the cartographer Frans Jacobszoon Visscher on an expedition in the ships Heemskerck and Zeehaen. One aim was to find the supposed island of Beach, said to have plentiful gold. They sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> on 14 August, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> on 5 September and left on 8 October into the Roaring Forties. On 24 November Tasman sighted the west coast of Tasmania and formally claimed the land on 3 December.</p><p>On 13 December 1642 he sighted the north-west coast of the South Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. He believed it was the western side of the long-imagined Southern Continent. At what is now Golden Bay, Māori paddled out in a waka and clubbed four sailors to death; the archaeologist Ian Barber suggests that local Māori were trying to protect a cultivation field under ritual protection. The expedition fired on the waka, and Tasman named the place Murderers' Bay. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> on 15 June 1643.</p><p>His second voyage, begun on 30 January 1644 with the Limmen, Zeemeeuw and Braek, followed the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, missed the Torres Strait and mapped the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The company judged his explorations a disappointment, because he had found neither a promising trading area nor a useful route, and chose to seek a more persistent explorer next time. He became a member of the Council of Justice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in 1644, retired in 1653, and died there on 10 October 1659. His journal was not published until 1898.</p><p>His voyage showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> was not joined to a larger Southern Continent, though cartographers drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> as its west coast until Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> circumnavigated it in 1769. Tasmania, the Tasman Sea and the Abel Tasman National Park are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abel Tasman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>European accounts, from <b>Abel Tasman</b>’s visit in 1643 to James Cook’s voyages of the 1770s and William Mariner’s narrative of 1806–1810, recorded the realm’s ceremonies and politics late in its long ascendancy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The first European to arrive, <b>Abel Tasman</b>, left in 1642 after a skirmish in which four of his men and at least one Māori were killed.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maori">Māori · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch navigator <b>Abel Tasman</b> sighted the west coast of New Zealand's South Island, the first recorded European sighting.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-13">13 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
